WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.595
Ever wondered why running


00:00:01.645 --> 00:00:03.240
an AI model like


00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:04.885
Llama 70B makes your


00:00:04.935 --> 00:00:06.530
GPU scream, while a


00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:08.175
7B model runs effortlessly


00:00:08.225 --> 00:00:10.120
on a laptop? It


00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.765
all comes down to


00:00:11.815 --> 00:00:13.710
model weights. In modern


00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.355
AI, weights are the


00:00:15.405 --> 00:00:17.000
learned numerical parameters stored


00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:18.645
in memory that determine


00:00:18.695 --> 00:00:20.590
how neural activations flow.


00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.235
A 7-billion parameter model


00:00:22.285 --> 00:00:23.880
literally holds seven billion


00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:25.825
distinct floating point numbers.


00:00:25.875 --> 00:00:27.470
Here is the exact


00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.415
formula. At standard 16-bit


00:00:29.465 --> 00:00:31.060
precision, every single parameter


00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:32.705
requires two bytes of


00:00:32.755 --> 00:00:34.650
storage. That means a


00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.295
70-billion parameter model demands


00:00:36.345 --> 00:00:37.940
a staggering 140 gigabytes


00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:39.585
of ultra-fast VRAM just


00:00:39.635 --> 00:00:41.230
to load the weights


00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.175
into GPU memory. During


00:00:43.225 --> 00:00:44.820
inference, text generation is


00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:46.765
strictly memory-bandwidth bound. For


00:00:46.815 --> 00:00:48.410
every single token generated,


00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.055
the GPU must stream


00:00:50.105 --> 00:00:51.700
every active weight from


00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.345
VRAM into the compute


00:00:53.395 --> 00:00:55.290
cores. More weights mean


00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:56.935
more gigabytes transferred per


00:00:56.985 --> 00:00:58.880
second. This is why


00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:00.825
quantization is revolutionary. By


00:01:00.875 --> 00:01:02.470
compressing weights from 16-bit


00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.115
down to 4-bit, we


00:01:04.165 --> 00:01:05.760
slash memory requirements by


00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:07.405
four times, enabling massive


00:01:07.455 --> 00:01:09.050
models to run on


00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.695
consumer hardware with virtually


00:01:10.745 --> 00:01:12.640
zero loss in intelligence.


00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:14.285
Now you know: model


00:01:14.335 --> 00:01:15.930
weight size and precision


00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:17.575
directly dictate your compute


00:01:17.625 --> 00:01:19.520
costs. Follow for more


00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:21.465
deep-dive AI engineering breakdowns!

